{"id":"nasa-8yg8zd","src":"nasa","t":"APOD: 2026 November 24 – Galaxies Battle in the NGC 7771 Group","u":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-24-galaxies-battle-in-the-ngc-7771-group/","at":"2026-11-24T05:05:00.000Z","by":"","img":null,"body":[{"p":["APOD APOD"]},{"p":["Astronomy Picture of the Day"]},{"p":["Discover the cosmos! Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer."]},{"img":{"src":"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/NGC7771group_Marinoni_1179.jpg?w=1179&h=1422&fit=clip"}},{"h":"Galaxies Battle in the NGC 7771 Group"},{"p":[{"b":"Explanation:"},"After this galaxy battle, only one galaxy will remain. Already visible in the ",{"a":"https://app.astrobin.com/u/Bluesky71?i=vn4g73","t":"featured picture"}," are ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-june-1-ugc-1810-wildly-interacting-galaxy-from-hubble/","t":"rings"}," and faint star ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-may-17-ngc-1316-after-galaxies-collide/","t":"swirls"}," from previous ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2013-may-14-galaxy-collisions-simulation-vs-observations/","t":"gravitational encounters"},". Known collectively as the ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-august-31-ifn-and-the-ngc-7771-group/","t":"NGC 7771 Group"},", these galaxies lie almost 200 million ",{"a":"https://chandra.harvard.edu/resources/faq/astrophysics/astrophysics-18.html","t":"light-years"}," away toward the high-flying ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pegasus_(constellation)","t":"constellation Pegasus"},". The largest galaxy, ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barred_spiral_galaxy","t":"barred spiral"}," ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7771","t":"NGC 7771"}," on the lower right, is itself about 75,000 light-years across, and the galaxy on the upper left is spiral ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7769","t":"NGC 7769"},", which is almost as large. As these and other ",{"a":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_galaxy","t":"smaller galaxies"}," make repeated close passages, they will ",{"a":"https://i.pinimg.com/736x/3b/fd/fe/3bfdfefb4f45fc0d90af3a111e2704b5.jpg","t":"all likely merge"}," into one very large galaxy. Playing out over billions of years, the ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-highlights/galaxy-details-and-mergers/","t":"merger process"}," is understood to be a normal part of the ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/evolution/","t":"evolution of galaxies"},". Studying ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-january-6-colliding-spiral-galaxies-from-webb-and-hubble/","t":"galaxy clashes"}," like this helps ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-august-18-human-as-spaceship/","t":"humanity"}," to better understand the ",{"a":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-025-02563-1","t":"possible"}," merger ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-june-6-milky-way-galaxy-doomed-collision-with-andromeda-pending/","t":"between"}," our own ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/%22","t":"Milky Way"}," and ",{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-november-15-andromeda-and-friends/","t":"Andromeda"}," galaxies."]},{"p":[{"b":"Tomorrow’s picture:"},"open space"]},{"more":true},{"p":[{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/random-apod/","t":"Random APOD Generator"},{"a":"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-23-the-emergence-of-dark-nebula-lynds-1251/","t":"Yesterday’s Image APOD: 2026 November 23 – The Emergence of Dark Nebula Lynds 1251"},"Tomorrow’s Image"]}]}
